From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
Date: Sun, 23 Jan 94 20:12:38 PST
Subject: [#94-1-451] GOAL:  Reduce sleep time

I have found daily exercise to reduce the amount of time spent waiting to
fall asleep, and to increase the quality of the sleep I do get.  (I ride
my bicycle to and from work, a ~11 mile roundtrip over flat ground.)

Choline supplements also make some difference, especially as part of a
well-considered megavitamin regimen.  I find KAL Multi-Four + to have
most of the substances I want, thus keeping my pill count under 10/day.
Magnesium supplements are also reputed to help one dream more, though I
cannot say with certainty that I experience more than a placebo effect.

I still find I must sleep 8-9 hours a night for peak functioning, but since
I normally sleep more this is an improvement.  My body works fine on much
less, but since my job involves difficult thought I must take care of my
brain also.

While in college I tried lengthening my days.  No matter how long I stayed
up I only needed about 10-12 hours of sleep afterwards.  During "nighttime"
(when I would normally sleep) I experienced a time of chills, reduced 
energy, and lower metabolic rate, which went away after some time but 
still left me with sub-optimal brain function in the "morning".  If deep
thought is not at a premium this may be OK.

For a less extreme example, try a 26-28 hour day.  The increased hours
leave me almost fully functional.  Sadly, my workplace is not quite
progressive enough to allow me to stick to such a schedule, though it would
give me an extra 2-4 hours/day.

I find that a combined dose of piracetam/centrophenoxine will extend the
useful life of my brain for 3-4 hours on a late night, unlike caffeine
which just gives me jitters.  Nothing I have tried will postpone mental
tiredness any further.  Centrophenoxine is chemically related to DMAE
(I believe it breaks down into such over time), but I have not tested
DMAE in this way.  Maybe you should, since it's available at any reasonable
vitamin store.

Rumor:  I have heard that it is possible to accustom oneself to taking
15-minute naps every two hours, with no longer sleep periods at all.  I
may try this if I can get a good place to sleep undisturbed at work, but
I somehow doubt this.

